From b58f6830b60f988580861a0277c1eadd3b7afc9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?L=C6=B0u=20Oa=20Oa?=
 <212658678+luuoaoa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 15:59:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Local accounts: create the password factor with the user,
 openFyde-style

The account-creation powerwash on official images is upstream's own
trapdoor: AuthSessionAuthenticator::DoCompleteLogin creates a brand-new
persistent user with NO auth factor ("password setup would happen later
in OOBE flow"), while UserDirectoryIntegrityManager records the user
with CleanupStrategy::kSilentPowerwash because the first user of a
device can become its owner. From the moment the username is submitted
until LocalPasswordSetupScreen stores the factor minutes later, any
Chrome restart makes MisconfiguredUserCleaner call
SessionManagerClient::StartDeviceWipe() -- and if the wipe is refused
in-session it RequestRestart()s and fires again from the login screen,
which is exactly the crash/reboot/reboot/powerwash the ThinkPad showed
the moment a new local username was entered.

This adopts the openFyde local-account shape (studied from their public
R144 patchset) instead of test switches:

* The local-account OOBE screen now collects the password (+confirm)
  next to the name, validated to the same 8-code-point minimum as
  PasswordFactorEditor, with vi translations for the new strings.
* The screen puts the password on the UserContext as both the Key
  (labeled local-password) and the LocalPasswordInput.
* DoCompleteLogin, for a new persistent AccountType::LOCAL user with a
  password, appends AddLocalPasswordFactor + RecordFirstAuthFactorAdded
  to the same operation chain that creates and mounts the cryptohome:
  the account is born with its factor and the kSilentPowerwash window
  never opens. AddLocalPasswordFactor goes through
  AuthFactorEditor::SetPasswordFactor, i.e. the exact factor that
  LocalPasswordSetupScreen would have written (local-password label,
  local metadata, system-salt hash), so later pod login is unchanged.
  PasswordSelectionScreen sees the configured factor and exits
  NOT_APPLICABLE on its own.
* LoginPerformer skips the trusted-settings wait and the online
  allowlist check for local accounts (openFyde parity; also keeps login
  working while consumer ownership is still being established).
* SaveForceOnlineSignin is a no-op for local accounts, OnAuthFailure
  never routes them to online reauth, and a token-status check can no
  longer flip a local pod to ONLINE_SIGN_IN.
* The policy test switches baked into chrome_main_delegate
  (--allow-failed-policy-fetch-for-test,
  --disable-policy-key-verification, --profile-requires-policy=false,
  --skip-force-online-signin-for-testing) are removed. Local users get
  no user cloud policy manager at all via the existing
  !user->HasGaiaAccount() bail-out, and device ownership is taken by
  the stock consumer OwnerSettingsService path, which is what
  session_manager's DevicePolicyService::Initialize expects to find on
  the next boot. --disable-gaia-services, the HID-detection skip, the
  dummy API keys and --cros-region=vn stay.
